  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and seasonably cool temperatures persist \n   through the rest of the week and into early next week.\n\n-  Critical fire weather continues for Southwest Montana today \n   due to gusty winds and low humidity.\n\n-  Widespread heavy rain in North-Central Idaho late tonight \n   through Friday morning.\n\nA broad upper-level low over the Pacific Northwest will maintain \ncool and unsettled conditions across the region today. The threat \nfor strong thunderstorms is minimal compared to Wednesday, though\nscattered light rain showers and a couple thunderstorms will \npersist through the afternoon. A stronger shortwave disturbance\nand elongated cold front rotating around the parent low will \nadvance eastward late tonight into early Friday morning, \nsignificantly increasing shower coverage and rain rates. North- \nCentral Idaho will receive the heaviest precipitation, with a near\n100 percent probability of rainfall amounts in the 0.25 to 0.50 \ninch range, and a 60 percent probability of exceeding 0.75 inches \nalong and east of US Highway 95 through Friday morning. \nPrecipitation amounts will taper spatially heading north into \nNorthwest Montana and east toward the Continental Divide.\n\nSouthwest Montana will remain the most important exception to \nwidespread wetting rains today into Friday. Strong southwesterly \ngradient winds and low relative humidity will sustain critical \nfire weather conditions through Thursday evening, particularly \nacross Fire Zone 111. Elevated fire weather conditions will linger\ninto Friday before the primary cold front crosses the Divide.\n\nShowers and cool conditions will spread into western Montana\nFriday morning as the upper low approaches. Persistent\nsouthwesterly flow aloft will keep temperatures near to slightly\nbelow early-fall averages through the weekend. Ensemble guidance \ncontinues to support the slow-mocing Pacific Northwest closed low\nejecting across the Northern Rockies Sunday into Monday, bringing\nanother surge of widespread precipitation (50 to 70 percent \nchance of exceeding 0.25 inches) and reinforcing cool, seasonable \nconditions.",
